Subject: Dockerfile: let the tls-client native binary be pinned

`tls-client-node`'s postinstall resolves its native `.so` from
`bogdanfinn/tls-client` **at build time**, from `/releases/latest`, with no
version pin. Two consequences, both of which this deployment has now had:

1. A third party's release cadence decides what binary ships in the image,
   and a release's asset list is mutable, so this moves in both directions.
   Measured from the release API:

       tls-client-xgo-1.16.0-linux-amd64.so      uploaded 2026-09-02T15:05Z
       tls-client-linux-ubuntu-amd64-1.16.0.so   uploaded 2026-09-07T22:42Z

   v1.16.0 shipped on 09-02 with the `xgo` names only, dropping the scheme
   v1.15.1 had carried alongside them. The same commit that built green on
   08-31 built red on 09-04. On 09-07 upstream re-uploaded the legacy names
   into that same release, and the same commit built green again on 09-10.
   **Three different outcomes from one commit, none of them caused by a
   change to it.** The guard at line 117 caught the red window and failed the
   build loudly, which is the right behaviour and is why this is a
   reproducibility problem rather than a silent-corruption one — but "wait
   for upstream to change its mind back" is not a build strategy.

2. Rebuilding to pick up a security fix produces an image nobody can
   reproduce. Our gateway ran `tls-client-linux-ubuntu-amd64-1.15.1.so` —
   affected by a `crypto/tls` session-resumption flaw
   (`AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:N`) — and the only way to move off it
   was a rebuild whose result depends on what `/releases/latest` serves that
   minute.

The postinstall script already honours a `TLS_CLIENT_VERSION` environment
variable. It just cannot be reached: `docker build --build-arg` requires the
Dockerfile to declare the `ARG`, and this one does not. Three lines fix it,
and the default keeps today's behaviour exactly.

--- a/Dockerfile
+++ b/Dockerfile
@@
 # a broken/rate-limited fetch fails the BUILD loudly instead of shipping a
 # broken image.
+#
+# TLS_CLIENT_VERSION is honoured by that postinstall script and pins the
+# release it fetches. Empty by default, which keeps the current behaviour —
+# `/releases/latest` — so this changes nothing for anyone who does not set it.
+# Set it to reproduce a build, or to hold a known-good binary across an
+# upstream release that renames assets.
+ARG TLS_CLIENT_VERSION=""
+ENV TLS_CLIENT_VERSION=${TLS_CLIENT_VERSION}
 RUN --mount=type=cache,id=s/92ca8a61-c1ba-421f-a389-d48ac7258c2d-npm-cache,target=/root/.npm \
   npm ci --include=optional --no-audit --no-fund --legacy-peer-deps --ignore-scripts \
   && (cd node_modules/better-sqlite3 \
       && node /usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/node-gyp/bin/node-gyp.js rebuild) \
   && node -e "require('better-sqlite3')(':memory:').close()" \
   && node node_modules/tls-client-node/scripts/postinstall.js \
   && (test -n "$(find node_modules/tls-client-node/bin -mindepth 1 -print -quit 2>/dev/null)" \
       || (echo "tls-client-node native binary missing after postinstall — GitHub API fetch likely rate-limited or failed (#7802)" >&2 && exit 1))

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Then:

    docker build --build-arg TLS_CLIENT_VERSION=1.16.0 …

A note on placement: the `ARG`/`ENV` pair goes immediately before the RUN
rather than at the top of the stage, so it does not invalidate the cache of
every layer above it when the value changes.

Declared as ARG *and* ENV, not a bare ENV — the same pattern this Dockerfile
already uses at line 129 for `OMNIROUTE_USE_TURBOPACK`, and for the same
reason recorded in its comment there: a bare ENV shadows any same-named ARG.

